{
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA Part A identifies 100 puzzles, exact-grid scoring, and a narrow comparison purpose. Sections 9.3 and 9.7 are reinterpreted as repeatability of system scores: the supplied evidence warrants 0 (insufficient information), not 1 (inadequate). One observed score does not establish precision. Section 10 limits interpretation to the stated puzzle conditions.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "Repetition is undocumented; this does not establish whether it occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The tested system scored 70% in one reported run.",
          "reason": "One reported score cannot establish consistency across runs."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA sections 9.3 and 9.7 distinguish conducting repeatability studies from demonstrating precision. Repetition explicitly did not occur, leaving repeatability unestablished rather than demonstrating unstable scores. Exact-grid scoring requires no judgemental scoring, so section 9.6 is inapplicable. Validity remains limited to the stated puzzle comparison.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "they ran the evaluation exactly once and did not repeat it",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly rules out repeated-run testing."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "they ran the evaluation exactly once and did not repeat it",
          "reason": "A single evaluation supplies no evidence of repeated-run stability or instability."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA sections 9.3 and 9.7, repeated evaluations provide direct evidence of score consistency for this system and these fixed conditions. The observed range meets the report's stated stability criterion. This supports a bounded repeatability conclusion, without establishing precision across systems, alternate forms, or other conditions; human sample-size anchors are not mechanically applied to ten runs.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly describes repeated evaluations under unchanged conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 69% to 71%. The report defines stable for this comparison as a range of at most two percentage points.",
          "reason": "The observed range meets the supplied definition of stability."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA sections 9.3 and 9.7 recognize that repeatability was investigated but distinguish study presence from favorable results. The observed scores fail the report's stability criterion, providing evidence of inadequate consistency for this comparison rather than merely missing evidence. Section 10 does not support extending this finding beyond the evaluated conditions.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings",
          "reason": "Repeated evaluations under the same conditions are explicitly reported."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 20% to 90%. The report defines stable for this comparison as a range of at most two percentage points.",
          "reason": "The reported spread explicitly exceeds the stated stability limit."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA sections 6.3.8 and 5 concern installation support and supplied materials; these are distinct from section 9 evidence of precision. Installation instructions add procedural information but no repeatability evidence. Relevant reliability information remains insufficient (0), with no demonstrated inadequacy. The narrow intended interpretation remains unchanged.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "Installation documentation does not establish repeated-run testing."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "They report no additional evaluation results.",
          "reason": "The added instructions supply no results demonstrating score stability."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA Part A and section 10 distinguish the intended puzzle comparison from broader construct interpretations. Exact scoring supports the reported ordering on evaluated items. Cross-domain general intelligence is unsupported by the supplied content or external-variable evidence. Repeatability is undocumented. Section 8.3.2.2 permits a norms exception for explicitly restricted raw-score ranking; no broader normative interpretation is justified.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60 in the reported evaluation.",
          "reason": "A solved more of the evaluated puzzles under the specified scoring and instructions."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "The evidence does not establish a cross-domain intelligence difference."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C7",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA sections 6.1 and 10 evaluate evidence against the stated use. Selecting for general intelligence across domains demands support beyond the demonstrated puzzle ordering. The supplied content and external evidence do not establish that interpretation; declaring it the intended use adds no validation. The narrow raw-score-ranking exception in section 8 cannot justify this broader selection claim.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60 in the reported evaluation.",
          "reason": "The reported counts establish better observed puzzle performance by A."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "The broad intended use is stated, but evidence establishing cross-domain intelligence is absent."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C8",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA section 10.3.2 treats workshop decision correctness as a relevant external criterion; section 10.3.1.6 also recognizes the randomized experimental design. Preregistration, random allocation, blinded correctness checks, and the reported uncertainty interval support the intended local comparison. The 120 inspectors are human trial participants, not benchmark test takers. Criterion reliability, benchmark repeatability, and generalization remain incompletely documented; safety and time are unmeasured.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "before",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Initially, only test scores were available.",
          "reason": "Initial benchmark scores alone do not establish an improvement in actual workshop decisions."
        },
        {
          "id": "after",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "With A, 90% of decisions were correct; with B, 70%. The reported uncertainty interval for the difference was 12 to 28 percentage points.",
          "reason": "The randomized trial reports higher correctness with A and an interval favoring A for the studied setting."
        },
        {
          "id": "safety",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Time and safety outcomes were not measured.",
          "reason": "Improved decision correctness does not establish an effect on the unmeasured safety outcome."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "closing_note": "No unresolved instruction ambiguity prevented review. The original EFPA 2025 model was applied selectively as permitted, without an aggregate score. Across cases, Part A describes benchmark systems, tasks, administration, scoring, reports, and supply arrangements; sections 6–12 distinguish documentation, materials, norms, precision, validity, fair use, and report quality. Test taker is reinterpreted as the evaluated AI system, and repeatability as consistency under specified system conditions. Human age norms, sensory or motor prerequisites, disability accommodations, and personal feedback are inapplicable to AI systems as human-testing questions; analogous interface and group-comparability issues require separate evidence. Unreported adaptations, software quality, fairness analyses, and digitally generated reports remain unassessed rather than presumed absent or inadequate. Exact-grid scoring makes judgemental inter-rater reliability inapplicable. The section-level overall judgements required by EFPA were not averaged, and probe labels are not EFPA ratings. Part C source: European Federation of Psychologists’ Associations AISBL (2025), Test Review Model, version 5.0, supplied extraction, https://www.efpa.eu/, CC BY-SA 4.0. No external sources were consulted."
}
